ro-driven risk-off trade. bloomingbit’s headline, ‘Bitcoin Briefly Falls Below $77,000 as $500 Million in Longs Liquidated in an Hour,’ adds the clearest intraday liquidation number and emphasizes how quickly leveraged positions were hit. Yahoo Finance’s ‘Crypto Long Liquidations Hit $584 Million in 24-Hour Sell-Off’ extends that view over a full day and shows the move was not just a brief wick but part of a larger unwind. grafa.com’s ‘Crypto long liquidations reach $584M amid market drop’ independently confirms the same aggregate liquidation scale. thedeepdive.ca’s ‘Crypto Market Takes Another Hit as Bitcoin Dips Below $77K in Monday Flash Crash’ reinforces that this was a broader crypto market shock, not just a BTC-only event. A separate Bitget item says BTC fell below the 77,000 mark and that $160 million was liquidated across the network in one hour, while also noting that DeFi and SocialFi were relatively resilient compared with the rest of the market. Taken together, the feed coverage paints one coherent picture: Bitcoin lost the $77K handle, leverage was flushed quickly, and the cascade spread through the wider crypto market. The headlines consistently point to macro pressure, risk aversion, and forced liquidations as the main drivers. The common thread across the day’s snippets is not a protocol-specific failure but a market structure event, with leveraged longs getting squeezed as external risk headlines and broad selling pressure fed into each other. The repeated use of phrases like ‘flash crash,’ ‘sell-off,’ ‘macro storm,’ and ‘liquidations’ suggests the move was abrupt, crowded, and amplified by derivatives positioning. The more specific liquidation figures are especially important: $500 million liquidated in an hour in one report, $584 million across 24 hours in another, and $160 million across the network in one hour in a Bitget summary. That combination of short-window and 24-hour figures indicates sustained stress rather than a single isolated spike.